## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-tenant adapter design under strict hardware and privacy constraints; evaluates decision rationale for injection points, quantization, and evaluation rigor.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant adapter design and privacy guarantees
- Adapter types: LoRA, QLoRA, PEFT and their trade-offs
- Injection points: attention first, FFN fallback
- Quantization, step budgets, and gradient checkpointing
- Evaluation: latency, factual accuracy, privacy leakage tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-train step applying per-tenant adapters
function trainStep(batch, tenantId) {
  const baseOut = model.forward(batch.inputs);
  const adapOut = adapter[tenantId].apply(baseOut);
  const loss = lossFn(batch.targets, adapOut) + adapterReg(tenantId);
  optimizer.step(loss);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate tenant isolation in logs and metrics?
- What rollback strategy would you use if a tenant adapter regresses performance?

## Why This Question Matters
Provides a practical, beginner-friendly approach to assessing grounding, code fidelity, and relevance for a developer assistant across private documentation corpora with versioning support.

## Key Concepts
- Grounding responses to exact doc version and section
- Detection of code block hallucinations
- Metrics: precision@1, grounding coverage, and citation drift

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal runner skeleton
const prompts = loadPrompts('prompts-50.json');
prompts.forEach(p => {
  const ans = model.ask(p);
  const grounded = verifyGrounding(ans, p.docVersion, p.section);
  const hallucinated = detectHallucinations(ans, p.docVersion);

  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    prompt: p.question,
    grounded,
    hallucinated,
    precisionAt1: computePrecision(ans, p.expectedEndpoints)
  }));
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates how candidates design end-to-end data-fetch pipelines with planning, dynamic re-planning, and observability in a production analytics context.

## Key Concepts
- LangChain agent workflows and orchestration
- AutoGen planning, tool-use patterns, and multi-agent coordination
- Idempotent REST calls with cache coherence guarantees
- Dynamic re-planning on failures with rollback paths
- Observability: distributed tracing, latency metrics, and freshness validation

## Code Example
```javascript
function buildPlan(input) {
  const planner = new AutoGenPlanner({
    tools: [restTool, cacheTool, loggingTool],
    retryConfig: { maxAttempts: 3, backoffMultiplier: 2 },
    onFailure: 'replan'
  });
  return planner.createPlan({ input, phases: ['fetch', 'validate', 'cache'] });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to craft brand-isolated PEFT strategies with governance for multilingual, cross-brand assistants.

## Key Concepts
- PEFT, LoRA, QLoRA; per-brand adapters; versioned KB drift; privacy and data governance; latency.

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo-injection outline for adapters
function injectAdapters(model, brand){
  model.addAdapter('attn', {rank:8, alpha:32, type:'LoRA', quant:4});
  model.addAdapter('ffn', {rank:8, alpha:32, type:'LoRA', quant:4});
  model.selectBrand(brand);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor drift and roll back adapters? 
- How would you audit data flows per brand?

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design a scalable, low-latency RAG architecture for security documentation, emphasizing chunking strategies, embedding selection, and performance optimization under strict SLAs.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented generation systems
- Vector database indexing and upsert operations
- Document chunking granularity and semantic coverage
- Cross-encoder reranking for precision improvement
- Latency budgeting and fault tolerance mechanisms

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal indexing sketch (pseudo-code)
const chunks = chunkText(doc.text, 600);
for (const chunk of chunks) {
  await vectorDB.upsert(chunk.id, embed(chunk.text));
}
```

